1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,480
OrderClerk AI is a working counter desk for a small shop receiving written

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:08,961
customer orders. A casual message can name products inconsistently, omit a quantity, or

3
00:00:08,961 --> 00:00:13,441
ask for stock that is no longer available. The aim is to help

4
00:00:13,441 --> 00:00:17,922
a clerk move from that message to a reliable picking list while keeping

5
00:00:17,922 --> 00:00:22,402
uncertain orders in review. This demonstration uses fictional customers and a synthetic catalogue

6
00:00:22,402 --> 00:00:26,883
priced in Zambian kwacha. It is a local prototype, not a connected WhatsApp

7
00:00:26,883 --> 00:00:28,261
or production shop system.

8
00:00:29,261 --> 00:00:34,242
The clerk supplies a customer label, a stable message reference, and the original

9
00:00:34,242 --> 00:00:39,223
order message. The verified provider is Ollama running the pretrained Qwen two point

10
00:00:39,223 --> 00:00:44,204
five, three billion parameter model. The model proposes order lines and identifies unsupported

11
00:00:44,204 --> 00:00:49,184
products. Application code checks exact catalogue aliases and verifies explicit quantities next to

12
00:00:49,184 --> 00:00:54,165
their products in the original message. The quantity guard supports digits and English

13
00:00:54,165 --> 00:00:59,146
one through ten. Other or ambiguous phrasing is routed to review instead of

14
00:00:59,146 --> 00:01:00,679
receiving an invented number.

15
00:01:01,679 --> 00:01:05,970
The message asks for two bags of rice and one bottle of oil.

16
00:01:05,970 --> 00:01:10,261
The resulting order contains exactly those lines, using stored prices of fifty five

17
00:01:10,261 --> 00:01:14,552
kwacha per rice bag and forty five per oil bottle. The total is

18
00:01:14,552 --> 00:01:18,844
one hundred and fifty five kwacha. Stock falls from ten rice bags to

19
00:01:18,844 --> 00:01:23,135
eight, and from three oil bottles to two. The picking list can be

20
00:01:23,135 --> 00:01:27,426
downloaded as CSV. This screen also shows replay: submitting the same message reference

21
00:01:27,426 --> 00:01:30,727
returns the original order without reserving stock a second time.

22
00:01:31,727 --> 00:01:36,223
The next message says, I want rice. It contains no requested quantity, so

23
00:01:36,223 --> 00:01:40,719
the system asks how many bags are required and creates no order or

24
00:01:40,719 --> 00:01:45,215
reservation. This behavior was not assumed. An early model call guessed a quantity

25
00:01:45,215 --> 00:01:49,711
of one, which led to the source grounding guard. A model suggestion is

26
00:01:49,711 --> 00:01:54,207
accepted only after the application finds a valid explicit quantity for the product.

27
00:01:54,207 --> 00:01:58,702
This conservative boundary is useful because an invented number can become a real

28
00:01:58,702 --> 00:01:59,394
picking mistake.

29
00:02:00,394 --> 00:02:06,059
Here the customer asks for five oil bottles. Only two remain after the

30
00:02:06,059 --> 00:02:11,725
first order, so the application reports the shortage and leaves stock unchanged. The

31
00:02:11,725 --> 00:02:17,390
same review path handles unsupported products. A normalized SQLite database separates customers, incoming

32
00:02:17,390 --> 00:02:23,055
messages, products, aliases, orders, line items, stock movements, reservations and events. Integer minor

33
00:02:23,055 --> 00:02:28,720
units avoid floating point money errors. A write transaction serializes stock decisions so

34
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:32,643
two requests cannot both reserve the same last units.

35
00:02:33,643 --> 00:02:39,229
Nine automated tests cover the workflow, replay, concurrent stock protection, HTTP interface, source

36
00:02:39,229 --> 00:02:44,816
grounding and a database upgrade that preserves saved orders. Four real model scenarios

37
00:02:44,816 --> 00:02:50,402
passed: clear order, missing quantity, unsupported product and shortage. The repository contains the

38
00:02:50,402 --> 00:02:55,989
scripts and observed results. This small synthetic set is not a general accuracy

39
00:02:55,989 --> 00:03:01,575
benchmark, and no time savings or sales impact has been measured. OpenAI Codex

40
00:03:01,575 --> 00:03:07,162
assisted with implementation and documentation, and this narration is synthetic. A separately labeled

41
00:03:07,162 --> 00:03:12,748
offline parser is available; an optional OpenAI integration remains unverified. The next step

42
00:03:12,748 --> 00:03:16,186
is shop owner feedback and broader language testing.
